<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="faq">
  <title>Frequently Asked Questions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Below are answers to the questions that students ask most often. If your
      question is not answered here, please ask me in class or during office
      hours.
    </p>

  
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-participation">
    <title>Is participation in class required?</title>
    <p>
      Participation in class is a basic expectation from students, and all of
      the in-class discussions are testable material.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-missed-class">
    <title>I missed a day of class. What should I do?</title>
    <p>
      The lecture notes are posted on the
      <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Mehdi/shorttitlelowercase.html">MATH 13 course website</url>,
      which you can use to catch up with the class. If you then have any
      questions at all about the material, please see me in the office hours.
      Also, remember that we have assignments due every week; check Camino and
      Gradescope to make sure you are not missing any due dates.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-missed-exam">
    <title>What if I have to miss an exam or am sick for an exam?</title>
    <p>
      By department policy, I am unable to give late (or early) exams. If you
      are too sick for an exam, you must notify me of your illness
      <em>before the exam</em> and, if possible, provide an official doctor's
      note excusing you from the exam. If you are an athlete and will be at a
      team event the day of a midterm, you must meet with me in advance to make
      arrangements. If your absence is not excused, and you miss an exam, you
      will receive a 0 on the exam. Note that if you miss all midterms (even
      with excused absences), you cannot pass the course.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-questions-outside-class">
    <title>How can I ask you questions outside of class?</title>
    <p>
      I can answer your questions during my regularly scheduled office hours.
      Extra appointments can be requested if, for example, all of the office
      hours during a week conflict with your class schedule. Any extra
      appointments must be requested <alert>at least 24 hours in advance</alert>.
      I do my best to accommodate reasonable requests; however, it may not be
      possible to accommodate all requests, in which case booking an
      appointment at the
      <xref ref="mlc" text="title"/> is a great solution.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-email-math">
    <title>Can I email you MATH questions?</title>
    <p>
      Please do not email me questions concerning homework or the material in
      class. Email is the worst medium to convey math, especially calculus, so
      please come to my office hours to discuss such questions. Suppose that
      all of the office hours during a week conflict with your class schedule,
      and that you tried to book an appointment and we did not manage to find a
      time that suits both our schedules; then you can email me your questions
      and I will do my best to answer them via email. Again, in such cases
      (that are rare), booking an appointment at the MLC is a better solution.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-performance">
    <title>How can I discuss my performance in the class with you?</title>
    <p>
      Please come to my office hours or request a private appointment to
      discuss this in person. Please do not email me to discuss your grade or
      your performance in the class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="faq-study-resources">
    <title>Are there some resources that you can provide or suggest to study for this course?</title>
    <p>
      The answer to this question will really depend on the individual. Having
      said so, here is a list of some of the resources (in no particular order)
      that can help you study:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            My lecture notes, which are posted on the
            <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Mehdi/shorttitlelowercase.html">MATH 13 course website</url>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Examples solved in class. Note that we solve different examples in
            class than the ones in the textbook, so that you have more solved
            problems with detailed solutions for studying outside the class. I
            recommend that first you try to solve them without looking at the
            solutions, then you can compare your work with the solutions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><p>Examples solved in the body of the (optional) textbook.</p></li>
        <li><p>The written homework problems. We will have 10 written assignments.</p></li>
        <li><p>For each topic, I have also suggested several optional problems to solve.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If you have questions, you can attend our regularly scheduled office
            hours, book an appointment to meet with me, or you can take advantage
            of the <xref ref="mlc" text="title"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
